      "hn_id": "50036869",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50036869",
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how eBPF hooks into kernel DRM ioctl calls to monitor per-pod accelerator usage. You will learn how to efficiently associate hardware device calls with Kubernetes container cgroups.",
      "authors": [
        "jrzayev"
      ],
      "content": "Tracking graphics accelerator usage at the container level has historically required intrusive userspace sidecars or vendor-specific daemonsets.\n\nKubeNPU solves this observability bottleneck by attaching an eBPF fentry program directly to the Linux kernel function drm_ioctl. Because every Direct Rendering Manager and accelerator driver funnels userspace execution through this entry point, the probe intercepts all hardware submissions at the lowest common denominator.\n\nThe in-kernel filter discards irrelevant ioctls before they ever touch userspace, reducing event collection overhead by more than fifty percent. When a submission passes the filter, the eBPF program pushes the kernel cgroup identifier into a ring buffer. The Go userspace daemon then translates that identifier into the corresponding pod name via containerd container runtime state.\n\nRebuilding the cgroup lookup index immediately upon a cache miss ensures newly scheduled workloads are captured on their very first accelerator event.\n\nClean kernel telemetry beats messy userspace instrumentation every single time."
    },
    {
      "title": "DuckDB speeds up recursive CTEs by retaining runtime state",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://duckdb.org/2026/08/25/how-duckdb-runs-recursive-ctes-faster",
      "date": "2026-10-10",
      "tags": [
        "catchup",
        "duckdb",
        "fixed-point-computation",
        "hn",
        "pipeline-scheduling",
        "query-execution",
        "recursive-ctes"
      ],
      "section": "databases",
      "interest_score": 8,
      "depth_score": 9,
      "utility_score": 8,
      "novelty_score": 8,
      "hn_id": "50037302",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50037302",
      "why_read": "Read this to understand the architectural shift in DuckDB that treats recursive CTEs as long-lived computations rather than repeated isolated queries, drastically reducing execution overhead.",
      "authors": [
        "Denis Hirn"
      ],
      "content": "Traditional analytical engines evaluate recursive Common Table Expressions by running the recursive step in an isolated loop, paying the penalty of pipeline scheduling, operator instantiation, and teardown on every iteration.\n\nDuckDB overhauled this architecture for version 2.0 by restructuring recursion as a single long-lived computation. Instead of rebuilding execution state per cycle, the query plan now owns the physical operator tree and maintains a reusable pool of pipeline executors. Iterations borrow these executors and retain epoch-invariant state like hash table builds across the entire fixed-point computation.\n\nThe engine also inspects exact frontier cardinalities dynamically before each step. It switches between lightweight inlined execution for tiny working tables and full scheduled worker threads when the frontier expands. This eliminates query dispatch overhead during the long tail of graph traversal queries.\n\nQuery engines often leak performance across iteration boundaries when they fail to distinguish persistent state from transient step inputs."
    },
    {
      "title": "Versioned filesystems provide transactional state for disposable sandboxes",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://www.shayon.dev/post/2026/283/versioned-filesystem-for-disposable-sandboxes/",
      "date": "2026-10-10",
      "tags": [
